  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1237 PM CDT Sat Sep 19 2026\n\nAn upper low will slowly translate from the Montana Rockies into \nMinnesota over the next couple of days. For now, it continues to \neject shortwaves into the northern Plains while sfc high pressure is \ntransitioning over southern Canada and the northern Great Lakes. \nThis has placed this forecast area in a region of weak easterly \nflow, helping to bump up low and mid level moisture. The associated \nlow clouds and drizzle or light rain are expected to persist in this \nregime through Monday morning. Adjusted overnight temps toward \nhigher hi-res guidance with smaller diurnal fluctuations expected \ndue to cloud cover. It's a known model bias to drag temps too low in \nthis type of pattern. \n\nAs the upper low exits to the east on Tuesday and a weak upper ridge \nbuilds in, expect daytime highs to rise on southerly flow to closer \nto normal values. On Wednesday evening, a llj sets up with some \nadditional shortwave energy as the upper ridge recedes. This means \nincreasing chances for showers and thunderstorms Wednesday night.",
